---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import editor from '../../assets/img/eaton-editor.png';
import confirm from '../../assets/img/eaton-confirm.png';
import writing from '../../assets/img/eaton-writing.png';
import monitor from '../../assets/img/eaton-monitor.png';
import monitorDark from '../../assets/img/eaton-monitor-dark.png';

// The short case. The deck is one sentence, the ending; the hero is the shipped editor cut to
// its first five rows. Five frames, each a region of one of the Webflow page's own images cut to
// the thing its paragraph names, and the weight follows the argument: the first decision has no
// figure of its own, since its evidence is the hero above it; the second carries two crops
// stacked under the words, the confirmation and the header while a write runs; the third, the
// page's biggest decision, carries the page's biggest pair, the dark draft and the shipped
// monitor at the same crop, two across the row, so four axes against one and the colours read at
// one glance. The canvas editor that lost the test isn't shown: only what launched can be, and
// the snapshot says so. The hand-off is a clause in Scope.
---
<Case
  title="Eaton — Kyle Xu"
  description="UX Design Intern at Eaton, June–August 2022. RapidLinkX, an IoT configuration tool for airport conveyor systems: a parameter editor and a fault monitor for airport engineers. In a comparative usability test the drag-and-drop editor was 35% faster and made 15% more errors, so the form shipped. 14 component variants added to Eaton's design system; in usability testing, operational efficiency +49.7% and error rates −36.8%."
  ogImage="/og-eaton.jpg"
  eyebrow="Eaton · UX Design Intern · June – August 2022"
  deck="The faster of the two editors we tested, 35% quicker, made 15% more errors, so the slower one shipped."
  next={{ title: 'Heygen', to: '/projects/heygen' }}
>
  <Fragment slot="claim">A configuration tool for the drives that run an airport's conveyors.</Fragment>
  <Figure slot="hero" src={editor} name="The editor" note="capture with placeholder rows" evidence="Shipped" loading="eager"
    alt="RapidLinkX's parameter editor: a blue header and toolbar with a Real-Time switch turned off and a Write In button, linked drives listed on the left with one selected, and a table of five starred parameters with a value field, range, default and display checkbox on each row"
    caption="The form that shipped, with the controls the next two decisions turn on: Real-Time, Starred, Compare and Write In." />

  <section class="col sec snap" aria-label="In thirty seconds">
    <dl class="facts">
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Scope</dt>
        <dd>RapidLinkX, an IoT configuration tool for the drives in airport conveyor systems, used by airport engineers: a parameter editor and a fault monitor over USB. I was the primary designer on the <span class="nb">day-to-day</span> product design, with my mentor on planning and communication across teams. The hardware was still being designed, regional stakeholders and an external development team had a say, and I added 14 component variants to Eaton's Brightlayer design system for the build. Under Eaton's NDA, only what launched is shown.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="meta">Outcome</dt>
        <dd>In usability testing of the shipped tool, operational efficiency up 49.7% and error rates down 36.8%.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-editor">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-editor">The slower editor</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>A wrong value on a baggage line stops the line. The comparative usability test split its participants between a <span class="nb">drag-and-drop</span> canvas and this form; the canvas was quicker and made more errors, and the form went forward. Some of the speed came back in two places, both in the frame above. Starred keeps the table to the parameters that matter on a check. Compare sets the live values beside a saved configuration or the defaults, so a drift shows as a difference rather than a number to hold in your head.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-write">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-write">Writing to a drive</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>An engineer on a routine round wants the tool linked and watching. One at a drive that has just failed wants nothing written until they've decided what.</p>
      <p>So <span class="nb">real-time</span> writing is a switch, and it's off when the tool opens. Turning it on, or pressing Write In, brings up the same confirmation, with a backup of the drive's current configuration already ticked. While a write runs, the header says so and asks not to be unplugged.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure narrow set="stack" caption="Write In's confirmation over the value fields, then the header while a write runs. The dialog's inverter is the drive."
        items={[
          { src: confirm, name: 'The confirmation', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The right end of the editor\'s toolbar with a Write In button, and beneath it a column of value fields with a dialog over them: Confirm Write-in, a note that changes will be written in real time to the connected inverter, a ticked checkbox to back up the config of the connected device, a config name reading DC 01, and Cancel and Confirm buttons' },
          { src: writing, name: 'The running write', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The right end of the editor\'s header and toolbar with a notice laid over them: a download icon, Writing to device, Please do not disconnect, and a Cancel button' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="col sec chap" aria-labelledby="c-monitor">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="c-monitor">The monitor, in colour</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The same test set a <span class="nb">text-heavy</span> monitor against a <span class="nb">colour-coded</span> chart, and with the chart participants found errors 40% faster. I drew the chart twice, light and dark, with the four channels stacked on axes of their own, and the dark one read better: the traces stood off the ground. The light one went forward anyway. It's what Brightlayer looks like, and the external developers were building from Brightlayer's components; a dark monitor would have meant a second theme for one screen. What the light one gave up in readability came back another way. By the time it shipped the four traces shared one axis, where a channel can be read against the others, and their colours had been <span class="nb">re-tuned</span> to stay apart once the lines crossed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="figs">
      <Figure set="pair" caption="Four axes on the dark draft; one axis, and the colours re-tuned, as shipped. The draft's labels are Chinese; the tool shipped in English."
        items={[
          { src: monitorDark, name: 'The dark draft', evidence: 'Concept', alt: 'The dark draft of the monitor, everything right of its sidebar: four coloured channel cards in blue, green, purple and orange with live values and four unset, four traces stacked on separate axes over a dark grid with a legend floating over them, and an overview strip beneath with the four traces overlaid' },
          { src: monitor, name: 'The monitor', evidence: 'Shipped', alt: 'The shipped monitor, the same region: Waveform Recording and Error tabs, channels 1 to 4 in blue, green, purple and orange with live values and min and max, channels 5 to 8 reading Please Select, the four traces on one axis over a hundred seconds, and an overview strip beneath' },
        ]} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="close">
    <p class="stance">A wrong value was the expensive thing.</p>
    <p>The form's lost seconds and the confirmation on every write are the price of keeping one out; the chart is there to find the ones that get in. In usability testing of the shipped tool, operational efficiency was up 49.7%, and error rates, the number I was watching, down 36.8%.</p>
  </Fragment>
</Case>
